29	# Define and publish the common inference audit event schema
30	
31	## Description
32	
33	This epic defines the common audit event schema for model inference traffic and publishes it so that both current and future emitting components (pipelines, workbenches) can produce compatible events. The schema specifies the structured fields every audit event must carry — caller identity, model and version, timestamp, and response status — and explicitly excludes payload content, consistent with the compliance requirement to record requests without capturing inference payloads.
34	
35	## Scope
36	
37	- Define a versioned audit event schema capturing: caller identity, model, model version, timestamp, and response status.
38	- Explicitly document that request/response payloads are out of the schema.
39	- Design the schema to be extensible so future components (Data Science Pipelines, workbenches) can emit compatible events without breaking existing consumers.
40	- Publish the schema in the appropriate component repository so consuming implementations and documentation can reference it.
41	
42	## Acceptance Criteria
43	
44	- The schema is defined, reviewed, and published in a component repository artifact that downstream implementations can build against.
45	- Every field required by the inference audit requirement (caller identity, model/version, timestamp, response status) is present in the schema.
46	- The schema carries no inference payload fields.
47	- The schema is extensible for adoption by non-inference components in future strategies.
48	- The schema is versioned.
49	
50	## HLR Traceability
51	
52	- **HLR-4** (P2): A common audit event schema is defined and published so future components emit compatible events.
53	- Note: this epic is the prerequisite for the inference emission requirement (HLR-1), which requires its events to conform to this schema; it is therefore prioritized as the highest priority of the HLRs it transitively enables.
