30	# KServe inference audit event emission and reliable delivery
31	
32	## Description
33	
34	This epic implements audit event emission in the KServe model serving stack so that every inference request through a KServe endpoint produces a structured audit event, and delivers those events to the cluster audit sink reliably. This is the core compliance capability: auditors get a structured record of who invoked which model endpoint, when, and with what outcome, delivered with at-least-once semantics to the cluster audit sink (tamper-evidence of the stored record is out of scope for this epic; see the decomposition summary's health warnings for human verification of that requirement).
35	
36	## Scope
37	
38	- Emit an audit event for every inference request routed through a KServe InferenceService endpoint, in both serverless and raw deployment modes.
39	- Each event captures caller identity, model and version, timestamp, and response status — no request or response payloads.
40	- Events conform to the published common audit event schema.
41	- Deliver events to the cluster audit sink (the standard cluster logging forwarder) with at-least-once semantics.
42	- Buffer events so they are not lost on component restart.
43	- Define the per-project audit enablement/disable configuration surface that the serving path honors, for use by administrators (surfaced in the Dashboard by separate work).
44	
45	## Acceptance Criteria
46	
47	- Every inference request through a KServe endpoint (serverless and raw deployment modes) results in an audit event containing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, and response status.
48	- No audit event contains request or response payload content.
49	- All events conform to the published common audit event schema.
50	- Events are delivered to the standard cluster logging forwarder with at-least-once semantics: an event may be duplicated but is never dropped.
51	- Events emitted shortly before a component restart are not lost — buffered events survive restart and are delivered.
52	- The per-project audit enablement/disable configuration is honored by the serving path: when auditing is disabled for a project, no audit events are emitted for that project's inference traffic.
53	- Build pipeline is green for the modified repositories.
54	
55	## HLR Traceability
56	
57	- **HLR-1** (P0): Every inference request through a KServe endpoint emits an audit event capturing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, and response status (not payloads).
58	- **HLR-2** (P0): Audit events are delivered to the cluster audit sink with at-least-once semantics and are not lost on component restart.
59	"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9afc93a15dd3fe65","agent_id":"afa1b5c98a7d3d61c","session_id":"d6022463-8a3e-4324-87fa-8d8b07f9d29c","uuid":"c8e6f4ae-9986-48e6-8f51-062cd5e407ef","timestamp":"2026-10-08T17:07:56.985Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8d82464e72fe8d32","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9010 decomposition"}
