32	# Document enabling and interpreting inference auditing
33	
34	## Description
35	
36	This epic authors the user-facing documentation for inference auditing: how administrators enable and disable auditing per project from the Dashboard, how inference audit events flow to the cluster audit sink, and how to interpret the event fields. This is substantive new content authoring (new documentation topics), distinct from the code implementation work.
37	
38	## Scope
39	
40	- New documentation topic(s) covering:
41	  - What inference auditing records (caller identity, model/version, timestamp, response status) and what it deliberately does not record (payloads).
42	  - How to enable and disable inference auditing per project from the Dashboard, including required administrator role.
43	  - How events are delivered to the cluster audit sink (standard cluster logging forwarder) and the at-least-once delivery semantics.
44	  - Reference documentation for the common audit event schema fields and how to interpret each field.
45	  - Guidance for compliance reviewers on verifying the audit trail.
46	- Follow the Red Hat docs content process and tooling for authoring and publishing.
47	
48	## Acceptance Criteria
49	
50	- Documentation covers enabling and disabling inference auditing per project from the Dashboard.
51	- Documentation covers interpreting audit events, including a field-by-field reference of the common audit event schema.
52	- Documentation notes that payloads are not captured and that delivery is at-least-once (duplicates are possible).
53	- Documentation is technically reviewed against the implemented behavior of the inference audit emission and the Dashboard controls.
54	- Documentation builds and publishes cleanly through the docs content pipeline.
55	
56	## HLR Traceability
57	
58	- **HLR-5** (P2): Documentation covers enabling auditing and interpreting events.
